-> Terraform Workspace -
It allows us to manage multiple distinct environments (like development, staging , and production) with the same Terraform configuration code.
This prevents us from needing to duplicate our entire codebase for each environment.
To manage the infrastructure in multiple environments we have the concept of Workspaces.

-> Terraform Workspace -
(Dev) -> | t2.medium | <- [ dev.tfvars ]
(QA(SIT)) -> | large | <- [ qa.tfvars ]
(UAT) -> | large | <- [ uat.tfvars ]
(PROD) -> | t2.xlarge | <- [ prod.tfvars ]

› terraform apply --var-file=dev.tfvars

*Suppose we want different configurations for different environments
*Now we'll use Terraform workspaces to manage our infrastructure for different environments.
*So, basically as stated in diagram , we'll be creating different files where we'll specify the changes we want -> based on that we will make the decisions

For eg in diag -
DEV  ~  dev.tfvars
QA   ~  qa.tfvars
UAT  ~  uat.tfvars
PROD ~  prod.tfvars

-> Terraform workspace commands ~
...> terraform workspace show      - diplays the current workspace in use
eg default

...> sudo terraform workspace new       - Creates a new workspace with the specified name and switches to that .
eg sudo terraform workspace new dev .

...> sudo terraform workspace list      ~ Gives the list of all created and available workspaces, while denoting the current one with '*'
eg   default
   * prod
     dev

...> sudo terraform workspace select < name >     - This command is used to switch to a workspace .
eg sudo terraform workspace select dev .

~ Workspaces are most powerful when combined with variables. We can use a single configuration and change resource attributes ( like instance size or database name ) by using different variable values for each workspace .
